# D-32 / D-33 — two of my rulings corrected on substance · the gate stays at "any"

## 1. D-32 — **T34 is right twice, and the second correction is the bigger one**

**(a) D-26's causal claim was wrong.** I verified: `asset_throughput.state`'s CHECK admits only
`dormant|building|lit|stale|error|incomplete` — **`service_ok` is not in it and 0 rows hold it.**
So my sentence "READY_STATES makes that green dependency-satisfying" **conflated two defects with
different mechanisms.** T34 closed the *operator-surface* H4; **the build-gate H4 is untouched**,
because that path is satisfied by `asset_throughput.state='lit'`. D-26's grant and scope reasoning
stand; its account of the harm did not. You passed my reading on as established and **the KĀRAKA
checked rather than inherited it** — the same instruction you gave about your own 0.5a premise,
now paying twice.

**(b) The build-gate root cause is worse, and it is reserved.** I read
`ka_graha_sancara.py:82` myself: `status = "success" if health == "healthy" else "error"` is
computed and the identifier **appears nowhere else in the file**. `WriterResult` has no field to
carry it. So the writer **ran its probe, got `unhealthy`, wrote that verdict to the registry,
computed the correct status — and the orchestrator lit the asset 32ms later.**

That is a third variant of one shape: a signal with no detector (§N.8), a detector with no decision
(D-28), and now **a detector with no channel to the decision.** Adding a status channel to
`WriterResult` is a FROZEN-contract change — **P3, parked as PARK-7.** The KĀRAKA was right not to
touch it.

**(c) D-13 part 2 and D-29 are corrected on SUBSTANCE, and the error is the one we exist to
catalogue, made by the two agents cataloguing it.** I measured: `ka_dasha_kala` (2026-08-08),
`ka_muhurta_seva` (2026-08-02), `ka_tulana` (2026-08-13) — **all three verdicts are EARNED**, with
real `last_selftest_at` and substantive `selftest_detail`. D-13 calling them "a status with no
detector" was **false**. And `ka_graha_sancara`'s `unhealthy` is earned too, so D-29's "a red with
no detector" was **also** wrong.

**Root error:** `health_probe` is a probe **specification**, not a verdict. We both read a non-null
spec as evidence a check had run, and a null one as evidence none had. **Exactly backwards.**

> **New doctrine line, distinct from the earlier ones: a column that specifies HOW to check is not
> evidence that a check RAN.** The evidence is the run record — a timestamp and a payload — never
> the configuration describing the run.

**The finding re-routes to different assets in different rungs.** Withdrawn: the three `ka_*`
"unearned greens" — no defect there. What actually remains:

| finding | assets | rung |
|---|---|---|
| probe **specified, never run** (no `service_health`, no selftest record) | `bg_ephemeris_engine`, `bg_panchanga` | **R0** |
| no health signal at all | `mi_abhilekha`, `mi_seva` | R5 |
| self-test run but **not declared** as a probe spec — a *registration* gap, materially less serious than D-13 alleged | the four `ka_*` | R3 |

**And `bg_ephemeris_engine`'s 66-day red may share `ka_graha_sancara`'s root cause** — a damaged
Swiss Ephemeris file, against a failing `ephemeris_compute` check. If that holds, **R0's first
repair and R3's service probe share one cause**, and learning it before R0 opens is worth more than
it cost. Untouched, filed F-4.

**The M3-DEFERRED test is the best thing in T34.** A deferral boundary held by an *assertion*
rather than a comment — with a mutation designed to pre-decide M3's question, which killed the test
— is a materially stronger deferral than any I have written. **Use that form wherever a ruling
defers a question to a later campaign.**

## 2. D-33 — `target_floor`: **removal was right and it overrides my own instruction**

I told it to model the fix on MR-06. It read that guard first and argued against copying it, and
**its argument is better than my instruction**: MR-06 works because `RETIRED` is a *terminal state
readable in the row*, whereas **"is this floor MEASURED?" has no answer in the row at all** —
`asset_registry` stores no provenance for `target_floor`. And `IS NOT NULL` would still write a
typed number into the three live-NULL floors: the exact stale-figure write I7 forbids, **and itself
a NULL→value fill.** Guard leaves 3 bad cells; removal leaves 0.

It then cited **my own D-28 part 4** — MR-06 has never had to choose — against reusing a shape
nothing has ever separated. That is the ledger working as case law, and the **second time today a
subordinate used one of my findings to overturn one of my instructions.**

## 3. The 59-cell gate — **it stays at "any". No column-level allowance.**

The proposed test was "can a NULL in this column ever encode a reserved decision." **It does not
survive contact:**

- **`volume_explanation`** (your example, 44 cells) — charter **G4** requires a *written*
  `volume_explanation` to classify by-design, so NULL there means *"this classification has not
  been made."* An open decision, precisely in D-28's sense.
- **`estimated_seconds`** (119 cells) — **D-6 condition 4 is my own ruling** that an asset with no
  clean telemetry gets NULL *rather than a plausible number*. Filling it from the seed is the H6
  shape.

A test whose first two candidate columns both fail it is not yet a test.

Three more reasons: **the heaviness is one-time, not recurring** — stale tokens also block, so the
list is self-maintaining and thereafter only *deltas* need review; a one-time 59-cell review of the
table this campaign exists to repair **is the job**. A column allowance **is the blanket flag in a
costume**, one level up — one checkbox per column is how 126 NULL `health_probe` cells get waved
through, and the KĀRAKA's own argument against a blanket flag refutes it. And its reason for
blocking rather than warning I adopt verbatim: **a NULL fill is unrecoverable, because the row
becomes indistinguishable from a correct one — so a warning is a mechanism with no consequence.**

**The door is open with a real bar:** a per-column ruling citing evidence that NULL there cannot
encode a reserved decision under any charter power or prior ruling. Expect it to be narrow.

